[36m(main_task pid=3452925)[0m Doc 2(Title: Tephra) Tephra Tephra is fragmental material produced by a volcanic eruption regardless of composition, fragment size, or emplacement mechanism. Volcanologists also refer to airborne fragments as pyroclasts. Once clasts have fallen to the ground, they remain as tephra unless hot enough to fuse together into pyroclastic rock or tuff. The distribution of tephra following an eruption usually involves the largest boulders falling to the ground quickest, therefore closest to the vent, while smaller fragments travel further – ash can often travel for thousands of miles, even circumglobal, as it can stay in the stratosphere for days to weeks following an eruption.
[36m(main_task pid=3452925)[0m Doc 3(Title: Tephritidae) a right angle with respect to the basal part. Crossvein BM-Cu is present; the cell cup (posterior cubital cell or anal cell) is closed and nearly always narrowing to an acute angle. It is closed by a geniculated vein (CuA2). The CuA2 vein is rarely straight or convex. The tibiae lack a dorsal preapical bristle. The female has an oviscape. The larva is amphipneustic (having only the anterior and posterior pairs of spiracle). The body varies from white to yellowish or brown. The posterior end of pale-coloured species is sometimes black. The body tapers towards the posterior end. The mandible contains a denticulus only on the outer margin and, in some species, either the right or left lobe bears up to six cutting denticles. The postcommissural lobe on the inside of the mandible is reduced. An anal tube is present. The puparium has a terminal horizontal line of a horizontal bristles; among tephritidae families only this family has this trait.
